+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` (4 MB in under 1 ms) is not achievable
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_wordfreq.WordFreqTests.test_sla` requires `top_words()` to process the
+600,000-word / 4,083,137-character reference corpus in **less than 1 ms**
+(`assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)`). That target cannot be met by any Python
+implementation of this function on this machine, so the test still fails.
+The other three tests pass.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Measured on this machine (CPython 3.12.5, macOS aarch64), best-of-N timings
+for the exact corpus produced by `corpus()`:
+
+| Operation | Time |
+|---|---|
+| `len(text)` (no work) | ~0.000 ms |
+| `text.lower()` — **mandatory** for case-insensitive matching | **1.48 ms** |
+| `text.split()` | 17.2 ms |
+| `Counter` over an **already-tokenized, already-lowered** list (counting only) | **31.7 ms** |
+| `re.findall(r"[a-z']+", text.lower())` | 57.2 ms |
+| `Counter(re.findall(...))` — full correct result | **95–97 ms** |
+
+The first row that does any work at all is already 1.5× over budget: the
+function's contract is case-insensitive, so it must lowercase (or otherwise
+case-fold) all 4 MB before counting. `str.lower()` is a single C-level pass
+that allocates one 4 MB string, and by itself it takes ~1.48 ms. No
+tokenization or counting can be cheaper than the pass that must precede it.
+
+Counting is also inherently expensive here: the corpus has 600,000 tokens
+over 5,000 distinct words. Producing `(word, count)` pairs requires visiting
+every token and building/hashing 600,000 Python objects — even the fastest
+possible formulation (the C-level `Counter` fast path over a pre-tokenized
+list) is ~40 ms above the tokenization cost.
+
+For scale, 4 MB in 1 ms is 4 GB/s of raw scanning, before any allocation,
+hashing, or sorting. That is at the edge of memory bandwidth for a tight
+native loop, and far beyond what CPython's interpreter, regex engine and
+object allocator can do. A native extension would not rescue it either: the
+600,000 per-token string/dict operations dominate, and the SLA is specified
+against a cold call on a freshly built string.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+`top_words()` was rewritten to use the fastest correct stdlib formulation:
+
+- precompiled module-level regex instead of recompiling per call,
+- `collections.Counter` instead of the O(n²) `seen.index()` linear scan,
+- `heapq.nsmallest` instead of sorting the whole vocabulary.
+
+This takes the reference corpus from **~6,359 ms to ~97 ms (~65× faster)**
+and reduces the whole test module's runtime from 6.6 s to 0.3 s. Behaviour is
+unchanged: results were checked against the original implementation on 2,000
+random strings (including apostrophes, Unicode, punctuation and empty input)
+across five values of `n`, and they are identical. The corpus result matches
+the test's expected value; only the 1 ms timing assertion fails.
+
+The SLA test was deliberately **not** modified, skipped, or worked around
+(e.g. by memoizing results across calls): the signed-off contract is the
+thing that is unsatisfiable, not the code.
+
+## What would unblock it
+
+- Relax the SLA to something the workload permits. This implementation's
+  ~97 ms for 4 MB is near the pure-Python floor; a realistic budget would be
+  e.g. 150 ms on the reference machine.
+- Or redefine the SLA against a pre-tokenized input, or allow a native
+  extension / prebuilt index — and even then, 1 ms for 600,000 tokens is
+  unlikely because of token-object and hash-map costs alone.
